#use(std/code)
#use(std/table)

#section{Extending YAMD}::

#section{Body readers (the extensibility primitive)}
	A function may register a #strong{reader} for its body, consulted
	#emph{before} parsing:

	#table(header: 1)
		#row
			#cell: Reader
			#cell: Behavior
		#row
			#cell: #code{markup} (default)
			#cell: Full YAMD parsing.
		#row
			#cell: #code{raw}
			#cell: Verbatim text. Block form: dedented, terminated only by dedent — no delimiter scanning at all. Inline form: brace-balanced scan, no #code{#} recognition.
		#row
			#cell: custom
			#cell: Plugin-supplied parser (tables, math notations, …).

	Examples:

	#code(lang: "plain")
		#code(lang: python)
			def f(): # not a comment, raw
				return {"x": 1}

		Inline #tt{x = 1} and math #m{sum_(i=0)^n i^2}.

	Context-scoped symbols keep the global symbol budget at one.

	#section{Fences for pathological inline content}
		Inline raw/markup bodies with unbalanced braces use alternative
		delimiters (Scribble-style): #code|={|{ … }|}=|, extensible fence:

		#code(lang: "plain")
			#tt|{ unbalanced } here }|
			#tt|==={ even }| inside }===|

		Opener #code|{|X{}| closes only at #code|{}X|}|. Block raw bodies never
		need this.

#section{Plugins}
	#list
		#item: A plugin is a host module exporting functions (+ optional body readers).
		#item: #code{#use(std/table, std/math: asciimath)} imports/binds; math notation (AsciiMath / LaTeX / typst-math) is chosen by the bound plugin — the core knows nothing about math.
		#item: Project-level #emph{reader macros} (e.g. backtick spans → #code{#tt{…}}) are allowed as opt-in sugar on top of existing functions; never new core forms.
